00:07:22.060 --> 00:07:25.930
Our customers access Ambrook
through our web, Android,

00:07:25.930 --> 00:07:29.860
and iOS apps, which are built in
TypeScript and React and React

00:07:29.860 --> 00:07:31.240
Native.

00:07:31.240 --> 00:07:36.250
These applications
talk to our web API,

00:07:36.250 --> 00:07:40.840
which is built in our monolithic
Next.js web application, which

00:07:40.840 --> 00:07:42.670
is written in TypeScript.

00:07:42.670 --> 00:07:46.900
We deploy that web app on
Google Cloud Run using Docker,

00:07:46.900 --> 00:07:50.620
and we have several
services configured

00:07:50.620 --> 00:07:53.020
for production, both
static and dynamic,

00:07:53.020 --> 00:07:55.340
as well as staging environments.

00:07:55.340 --> 00:07:58.280
Our load balancer sits in
front to direct traffic,

00:07:58.280 --> 00:08:01.130
deciding which request should
be passed to which services

00:08:01.130 --> 00:08:05.180
and passing others onto our CDN,
which is also run by Google.

00:08:05.180 --> 00:08:08.210
We spend a lot of time
optimizing for performance

00:08:08.210 --> 00:08:09.290
here.

00:08:09.290 --> 00:08:12.530
Next.js allows us to serve
static and dynamic content,

00:08:12.530 --> 00:08:15.260
server-side rendered and
statically generated,

00:08:15.260 --> 00:08:17.210
all in one application
where we get

00:08:17.210 --> 00:08:19.070
to make a decision
for each page what

00:08:19.070 --> 00:08:20.900
the right experience should be.

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:23.480
By pairing this with
Cloud Run services

00:08:23.480 --> 00:08:26.540
that are shaped to handle these
different kinds of traffic,

00:08:26.540 --> 00:08:29.480
we've been able to achieve great
performance across a variety

00:08:29.480 --> 00:08:32.350
of different request patterns.

00:08:32.350 --> 00:08:35.110
We use Firestore for
application data,

00:08:35.110 --> 00:08:38.140
Firebase Auth for authentication
and authorization,

00:08:38.140 --> 00:08:41.380
and Google Cloud Storage
for Blob Storage.

00:08:41.380 --> 00:08:45.560
Not everything we do can be done
in just the 200 milliseconds

00:08:45.560 --> 00:08:48.850
we aim to have all of our HTTP
requests return in though.

00:08:48.850 --> 00:08:51.550
To manage asynchronous
work, from receipt parsing,

00:08:51.550 --> 00:08:54.070
to data syncing, to
document generation,

00:08:54.070 --> 00:08:56.410
we use Google Cloud Task queues.

00:08:56.410 --> 00:08:58.600
We put workloads
on these queues,

00:08:58.600 --> 00:09:02.050
either with Cloud functions
or from the app itself.

00:09:02.050 --> 00:09:03.850
The task queues
schedule requests

00:09:03.850 --> 00:09:06.670
to be sent back to our monolith,
which holds all the business

00:09:06.670 --> 00:09:07.360
logic.

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:09.250
For example, when
a new transaction

00:09:09.250 --> 00:09:11.710
occurs on an Ambrook card,
we'd write a new record

00:09:11.710 --> 00:09:12.880
to Firestore.

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:16.240
That Firestore document creation
triggers a Cloud Function,

00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:18.070
which in queue is
a post request back

00:09:18.070 --> 00:09:20.440
to our monolith to
an endpoint that

00:09:20.440 --> 00:09:22.090
will send an email
notification to all

00:09:22.090 --> 00:09:25.930
the relevant parties based on
their notification preferences.

00:09:25.930 --> 00:09:28.960
This lets us have all the
business logic in one place

00:09:28.960 --> 00:09:31.960
and not have to
deal with deploying

00:09:31.960 --> 00:09:35.260
different versions of business
logic across different services

00:09:35.260 --> 00:09:37.930
or microservices.

00:09:37.930 --> 00:09:41.020
Now, there's a lot that goes
into running the Ambrook

00:09:41.020 --> 00:09:41.830
product.

00:09:41.830 --> 00:09:45.280
Our team uses a suite of
