tell us about your operation,

00:12:55.540 --> 00:12:57.820
and then if you want,
you can create an account

00:12:57.820 --> 00:12:59.800
to see your recommendations.

00:12:59.800 --> 00:13:04.390
That anonymous authentication
that happens right

00:13:04.390 --> 00:13:06.760
when they first
visit the page allows

00:13:06.760 --> 00:13:09.370
us to ask for
information just in time

00:13:09.370 --> 00:13:13.120
rather than putting up a
wall upfront before we get

00:13:13.120 --> 00:13:17.062
to ask them to tell us more.

00:13:17.062 --> 00:13:18.770
FILLIPO MADELLA: It
makes a lot of sense.

00:13:18.770 --> 00:13:22.150
Again, once more I think
you're prioritizing the user

00:13:22.150 --> 00:13:26.920
experience and also probably
is an audience that you want

00:13:26.920 --> 00:13:28.420
to make sure that
you're providing

00:13:28.420 --> 00:13:32.930
the friendliness of your
platform before anything else.

00:13:32.930 --> 00:13:35.080
And so if we talk
about the actual,

00:13:35.080 --> 00:13:37.570
the audience in
this industry, one

00:13:37.570 --> 00:13:41.440
of the things for GCP
users like our customers,

00:13:41.440 --> 00:13:43.330
is that at the end
of the day, we just

00:13:43.330 --> 00:13:47.020
want them to thrive in whatever
industry they're choosing.

00:13:47.020 --> 00:13:51.070
The Ag industry has evolved
over the years on the technology

00:13:51.070 --> 00:13:54.850
side but it seems that
you're innovating quickly,

00:13:54.850 --> 00:13:59.830
kind of outpacing usually the
industry's innovation itself.

00:13:59.830 --> 00:14:01.060
Do you think there is like--

00:14:01.060 --> 00:14:04.480
the way you're approaching
your technology decisions,

00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:05.650
is it helping with this?

00:14:05.650 --> 00:14:09.040
Or how are you innovating
so quickly on this?

00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:10.960
DAN SCHLOSSER: Yeah, absolutely.

00:14:10.960 --> 00:14:13.840
I think Ambrook is a company
that really could only

00:14:13.840 --> 00:14:15.370
be built today.

00:14:15.370 --> 00:14:19.870
Four years ago, there
was no Plaid, no Stripe.

00:14:19.870 --> 00:14:22.540
We're building this company
on top of fintech platforms

00:14:22.540 --> 00:14:26.650
that allow us to
offer an amazing user

00:14:26.650 --> 00:14:30.700
experience to a vertical
that has not really

00:14:30.700 --> 00:14:35.620
been built for in this way
by fintech before today.

00:14:35.620 --> 00:14:39.190
We are building on
top of these platforms

00:14:39.190 --> 00:14:41.950
because we don't want to
be building infrastructure,

00:14:41.950 --> 00:14:44.090
we want to be helping
our customers.

00:14:44.090 --> 00:14:46.090
And so building
on top of Google,

00:14:46.090 --> 00:14:48.640
building on top of Stripe,
building on top of Plaid,

00:14:48.640 --> 00:14:53.560
has allowed us to move
incredibly quickly and ship

00:14:53.560 --> 00:14:56.470
products that are actually
helping farmers today

00:14:56.470 --> 00:14:59.570
rather than spending what you
might have done five years ago,

00:14:59.570 --> 00:15:02.590
which is spend three years
building all the infrastructure

00:15:02.590 --> 00:15:05.357
and then start to build product.

00:15:05.357 --> 00:15:06.940
FILLIPO MADELLA:
Dan, it sounds great.

00:15:06.940 --> 00:15:10.690
I think the innovation
speed has a lot

00:15:10.690 --> 00:15:13.990
to do with how you're building
and the technology you

00:15:13.990 --> 00:15:15.380
are using, of course.

00:15:15.380 --> 00:15:18.490
So can we talk a little bit
about your deployment process,

00:15:18.490 --> 00:15:21.610
how you're approaching
and is there any insights

00:15:21.610 --> 00:15:23.140
that you would like to share?

00:15:23.140 --> 00:15:25.330
DAN SCHLOSSER: Yeah, absolutely.

00:15:25.330 --> 00:15:29.500
We really care about being able
to deploy quickly at Ambrook.

00:15:29.500 --> 00:15:30.940
It doesn't matter
how quickly you

00:15:30.940 --> 00:15:33.160
write the code if
you're constantly

00:15:33.160 --> 00:15:36.310
having to fix
breakages in production

00:15:36.310 --> 00:15:39.820
or run tests
manually to make sure

00:15:39.820 --> 00:15:41.740
that your latest
change isn't going

00:15:41.740 --> 00:15:43.190
to break things for customers.

00:15:43.190 --> 00:15:46.840
So we set up a testing
and automation suite that

00:15:46.840 --> 00:15:48.430
runs on every pull request.

00:15:48.430 --> 00:15:51.550
We use Google Cloud
Build and deploy

00:15:51.550 --> 00:15:55.120
each pull request in
GitHub, using GitHub Actions

00:15:55.120 --> 00:16:01.420
to a unique subdomain of
our Google Cloud Run staging

00:16:01.420 --> 00:16:02.300
environment.

00:16:02.300 --> 00:16:05.560
So each change, you can
confidently just click the link

00:16:05.560 --> 00:16:09.910
and go see what the change would
look like if it were deployed.

00:16:09.910 --> 00:16:12.820
We also build our React
Native application

00:16:12.820 --> 00:16:15.770
and have a QR code where
you can pull up your phone,

00:16:15.770 --> 00:16:18.910
scan that revision,
and any person

00:16:18.910 --> 00:16:21.250
that's reviewing a pull
request can click a link

00:16:21.250 --> 00:16:23.860
and see the web experience,
scan the QR code

00:16:23.860 --> 00:16:27.160
and see the Native
experience, and also see

00:16:27.160 --> 00:16:29.200
that all the tests are green.

00:16:29.200 --> 00:16:32.770
So when those pull
requests are merged

00:16:32.770 --> 00:16:36.580
we build the app, run
tests, and automatically

00:16:36.580 --> 00:16:39.700
deploy that to Google
Cloud Run without having

00:16:39.700 --> 00:16:43.730
to worry about any
manual deploying process.

00:16:43.730 --> 00:16:45.910
This is something we
invested in early.

00:16:45.910 --> 00:16:49.570
In my experience, once
you get into the heat

00:16:49.570 --> 00:16:52.060
of building product it
can be hard to prioritize

00:16:52.060 --> 00:16:52.930
infrastructure.

00:16:52.930 --> 00:16:57.610
So we did that first and
it has absolutely paid off.

00:16:57.610 --> 00:17:00.760
We deploy our web
application multiple times

00:17:00.760 --> 00:17:05.057
per day and our Android
and iOS apps every day

00:17:05.057 --> 00:17:06.849
and that's something
we're really proud of.

00:17:06.849 --> 00:17:08.891
FILLIPO MADELLA: Yeah, I
know exactly what you're

00:17:08.891 --> 00:17:11.755
saying, once you have
your first version of beta

00:17:11.755 --> 00:17:14.440
or of production-ready
application,

00:17:14.440 --> 00:17:16.960
you hardly find the time
or even the rationale

00:17:16.960 --> 00:17:19.420
to go back and change
your CI/CD process

00:17:19.420 --> 00:17:20.930
or it takes a lot of work.

00:17:20.930 --> 00:17:24.849
So I'm glad that you are
sharing that starting that early

00:17:24.849 --> 00:17:26.420
makes a lot of difference.

00:17:26.420 --> 00:17:28.750
And I'm sure you faced
a lot of challenges

00:17:28.750 --> 00:17:30.250
on the way, anything,
in particular,

00:17:30.250 --> 00:17:31.630
you would like to
highlight so we

00:17:31.630 --> 00:17:34.270
can share that it's
not all about roses

00:17:34.270 --> 00:17:37.537
but we have some thorns
as well along the way?

00:17:37.537 --> 00:17:38.620
DAN SCHLOSSER: Definitely.

00:17:38.620 --> 00:17:41.500
We definitely hit our
share of challenges.

00:17:41.500 --> 00:17:45.190
I would say that most
of them were around

00:17:45.190 --> 00:17:48.070
working with Next.js,
which especially when we

00:17:48.070 --> 00:17:51.460
were getting started was a
relatively new JavaScript

00:17:51.460 --> 00:17:52.300
framework.

00:17:52.300 --> 00:17:55.240
And had a fairly small community
of developers that were

00:17:55.240 --> 00:17:58.870
deploying it to Google Cloud.

00:17:58.870 --> 00:18:01.360
Our original application
used App Engine,

00:18:01.360 --> 00:18:05.230
that was the first thing that
I thought of to deploy Next.js

00:18:05.230 --> 00:18:07.390
because it was super
easy to set up.

00:18:07.390 --> 00:18:11.110
We had it up in
like an hour but one

00:18:11.110 --> 00:18:13.420
of Next.js' most
exciting features

00:18:13.420 --> 00:18:16.060
is incremental static
regeneration that

00:18:16.060 --> 00:18:19.990
allows us to statically
generate content at build time,

00:18:19.990 --> 00:18:23.860
and then when updates
happen, have them regenerate

00:18:23.860 --> 00:18:25.900
without having to redeploy.

00:18:25.900 --> 00:18:29.170
The thing is if you're
regenerating static content,

00:18:29.170 --> 00:18:31.240
you need to be able
to write it to disk,

00:18:31.240 --> 00:18:33.560
and App Engine doesn't do that.

00:18:33.560 --> 00:18:37.150
So we had an issue where we
were scratching our heads

00:18:37.150 --> 00:18:39.610
and saying why isn't
this content updating.

00:18:39.610 --> 00:18:42.958
And it actually had led to a 404
because we had moved something

00:18:42.958 --> 00:18:44.500
from one page to
another page, and we

00:18:44.500 --> 00:18:46.690
were like, why are
these 404s happening?

00:18:46.690 --> 00:18:50.740
And then we realized that
it's a read-only file system.

00:18:50.740 --> 00:18:54.890
So to fix that issue we moved
our application to Google Cloud

00:18:54.890 --> 00:18:55.390
Run.

00:18:55.390 --> 00:18:58.270
There was thankfully,
a pretty simple tool

00:18:58.270 --> 00:19:01.420
to auto-generate the
config for Cloud Run

00:19:01.420 --> 00:19:06.010
from an App Engine app, and we
were redeployed in a few hours

00:19:06.010 --> 00:19:08.620
and we've been on that
happily ever since.

00:19:08.620 --> 00:19:11.470
But yeah, it was a little
scary in the moment

00:19:11.470 --> 00:19:13.150
to be like, wait
a minute, we have

00:19:13.150 --> 00:19:16.600
to change our server
deploying platform?

00:19:16.600 --> 00:19:18.730
Like that's going
to take forever

00:19:18.730 --> 00:19:23.570
but it ended up being simpler
than we had originally thought.

00:19:23.570 --> 00:19:27.130
FILLIPO MADELLA: So
looking to the future, Dan,

00:19:27.130 --> 00:19:30.400
what are any technical
enhancements or anything

00:19:30.400 --> 00:19:35.980
that is on your roadmap
planned for the future?

00:19:35.980 --> 00:19:37.010
DAN SCHLOSSER: Yeah.

00:19:37.010 --> 00:19:40.610
Two big ones come to mind,
the first is task queues.

00:19:40.610 --> 00:19:44.060
As I mentioned, we
use Google Cloud Tasks

00:19:44.060 --> 00:19:45.780
for our asynchronous work.

00:19:45.780 --> 00:19:49.910
But today we use a single queue
for all of our different kinds

00:19:49.910 --> 00:19:52.130
of asynchronous tasks.

00:19:52.130 --> 00:19:54.770
In the future, we
think we will probably

00:19:54.770 --> 00:19:59.610
want to have a different
queue for each task type

00:19:59.610 --> 00:20:03.890
so that we can tune them
for the correct sort of QPS

00:20:03.890 --> 00:20:08.520
expectations, retry behavior,
and things like that.

00:20:08.520 --> 00:20:12.860
We also are operating
on a single production

00:20:12.860 --> 00:20:16.580
and then staging environment
in Google Cloud Run

00:20:16.580 --> 00:20:20.040
but our core app doesn't
understand the difference

00:20:20.040 --> 00:20:22.040
between these two
environments, and our database

00:20:22.040 --> 00:20:25.430
isn't set up separately for
different environments, which

00:20:25.430 --> 00:20:27.830
is something that we know
we're going to have to do.

00:20:27.830 --> 00:20:30.710
That will probably involve
creating a new Google Cloud

00:20:30.710 --> 00:20:35.660
project ID, and duplicating
all those services but yeah,

00:20:35.660 --> 00:20:37.255
we'll be doing that
in time I think.

